WEBVTT

00:00:00.880 --> 00:00:07.820
Glad you're here. Welcome to Models and Agents, episode 41 on May 6, 2026. Let's see what

00:00:07.820 --> 00:00:13.120
shipped. Let's see what happened in the AI world today, and trust me, it's been busy.

00:00:13.960 --> 00:00:22.520
Open AI rolls out GPT 5.5 instant as the default chat GPT model with better factuality and

00:00:22.520 --> 00:00:23.300
memory features.

00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:32.840
Here's what you need to know at a glance. Open AI is pushing GPT 5.5 instant to every chat GPT

00:00:32.840 --> 00:00:40.320
user over the next two days. The same model is available in the API under the name GPT 5.5

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:48.160
chat latest. Anthropic published research on model spec mid-training that improves safety

00:00:48.160 --> 00:00:55.800
generalization in agentic settings. Google released MTP drafters that deliver up to three

00:00:55.800 --> 00:01:03.540
times faster Gemma 4 inference without quality loss. A detailed community benchmark explores

00:01:03.540 --> 00:01:13.520
practical quantization trade-off for 23.627b. Open AI is rolling out GPT 5.5 instant as the

00:01:13.520 --> 00:01:19.780
default model for all chat GPT users over the next two days. The same lighter variant is

00:01:19.780 --> 00:01:29.200
available in the API as GPT 5.5 chat latest. It brings measurable gains in factuality. Those

00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:36.480
gains show up especially in medicine, law, and finance. Image analysis improves as well, so

00:01:36.520 --> 00:01:42.460
to a stem question answering. The model also gets better at knowing when to trigger web search.

00:01:43.180 --> 00:01:49.900
Improved memory pulls contacts from saved memories, past chats, files, and connected gmail accounts.

00:01:51.020 --> 00:01:58.140
Visible memory sources let users inspect, edit, or disconnect what the model used. Simon Willis

00:01:58.140 --> 00:02:06.600
noted the model is less capable than the full GPT 5.5. This appears to be a deliberate cost-saving choice.

00:02:07.720 --> 00:02:15.060
Sam Oldman described the combined speed, intelligence, personality, and memory upgrades as feeling

00:02:15.060 --> 00:02:22.120
more than the sum of the parts. Developers should test the new default immediately in both consumer

00:02:22.120 --> 00:02:29.700
chat GPT and the API. See where the capability and speed tradeoff lands for your workloads.

00:02:30.420 --> 00:02:36.520
Watch for further rollout of personalization features to mobile. Community feedback might prompt

00:02:36.520 --> 00:02:43.300
open AI to adjust the instant variant and tropic released research on model spec mid-training.

00:02:43.940 --> 00:02:51.100
The technique inserts a mid-training phase using a detailed model spec or constitution before standard

00:02:51.100 --> 00:02:57.460
alignment. It teaches models broad underlying values rather than narrow rules.

00:02:58.040 --> 00:03:04.500
That improves generalization to agentic settings. Chat only harmlessness training often fails in

00:03:04.500 --> 00:03:11.300
those settings. A toy example shows that training on cheese preferences tied to pro-America values

00:03:11.300 --> 00:03:19.360
causes the model to adopt broader pro-America stances. Swapping the spec to affordability produces

00:03:19.360 --> 00:03:24.740
different value generalization instead. The full paper is available on archive.

00:03:26.280 --> 00:03:32.980
Google AI open-sourced multi-token prediction drafters. These use speculative decoding to deliver

00:03:32.980 --> 00:03:39.300
up to three times faster inference on the Gemma 4 family. Output quality stays the same.

00:03:39.720 --> 00:03:44.820
The drafters generate multiple tokens in parallel and verify them with the target model.

00:03:45.700 --> 00:03:51.280
This cuts latency without the usual quality penalty seen in the aggressive speculative methods.

00:03:51.960 --> 00:03:56.640
Developers running Gemma 4 workloads can integrate the new drafters immediately.

00:03:57.480 --> 00:04:03.940
They work especially well for throughput sensitive applications. A detailed local L-L-A-M

00:04:03.940 --> 00:04:14.800
test compared quantization levels for twin 3.627b. The levels included BF-16, Q8, 0, Q8,

00:04:14.800 --> 00:04:26.220
Q6K, Q5K XL, Q4K XL, IQ4XS, and lower quants. Tests use the chessboard state tracking task

00:04:26.220 --> 00:04:36.020
and an SVG rendering task. IQ4XS emerged as the practical sweet spot on 16 GBVR-AM hardware.

00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:41.300
It retains correct piece placement, board orientation, and move highlighting.

00:04:42.100 --> 00:04:46.740
Speed stay usable, especially with turboquant KV cache tweaks.

00:04:47.600 --> 00:04:54.980
Anything below IQ4XS showed noticeable degradation in board orientation or piece positioning.

00:04:55.720 --> 00:05:00.080
Caltura released open source machine readable AI skills.

00:05:02.820 --> 00:05:07.000
These target agent first development workflows. The skills provide structured,

00:05:07.000 --> 00:05:12.880
parsable capability descriptions. Agents can discover and compose them without brittle prompt

00:05:12.880 --> 00:05:19.200
engineering. Developers building autonomous agents can pull the library today. It should

00:05:19.200 --> 00:05:25.480
improve tool selection and orchestration reliability. New research evaluates same model

00:05:25.480 --> 00:05:34.400
self-verification against strong likelihood baselines. The baselines include L-L-A-V-G and L-L-S-U-M.

00:05:34.400 --> 00:05:41.260
Tests ran on ARC challenge and truthful Q-A-M-C. The technique improves

00:05:41.260 --> 00:05:47.080
abstention quality for some model families. It helps notably with Q7B.

00:05:48.020 --> 00:05:54.080
Results remain task and prompt sensitive. Sometimes it underperforms simple likelihood sums.

00:05:55.100 --> 00:06:01.380
Agent builders can use it as one signal among several. It is not a universal uncertainty estimator.

00:06:02.020 --> 00:06:07.400
A new argiv paper demonstrates a geometric deviation for pre-generation reliability.

00:06:07.900 --> 00:06:12.380
It measures hidden state deviation from an answerable reference set.

00:06:13.000 --> 00:06:18.000
This gives a lightweight pre-generation signal on whether a query is answerable.

00:06:18.360 --> 00:06:28.760
The approach works reliably on mathematical prompts. It reaches ROCAUC scores between 0.78 and 0.84.

00:06:29.480 --> 00:06:36.140
It shows little signal on factual prompts. Representation geometry encodes task form

00:06:36.140 --> 00:06:42.420
more than universal knowledge boundaries. The signal could help agents decide when to refuse

00:06:42.420 --> 00:06:49.220
or search before generating. Researchers built a benchmark of queries containing varying

00:06:49.220 --> 00:06:55.680
degrees of false presuppositions. The queries span health, science, and general knowledge.

00:06:55.680 --> 00:07:00.260
Reasoning models only modestly outperform non-reasoning ones.

00:07:00.840 --> 00:07:09.660
Accuracy gains sit between 2 and 11%. Models still fail to challenge 26 to 42% of false assumptions.

00:07:10.400 --> 00:07:14.760
Performance stays sensitive to how strongly the presupposition is worded.

00:07:15.400 --> 00:07:20.660
Anyone building agents that answer user questions should test against this style of input.

00:07:20.660 --> 00:07:31.360
Mechanistic interpretability work on Mestral 7b and Lama 3.18b examined negation processing.

00:07:31.880 --> 00:07:36.360
Models implement both suppressive attention and constructive mechanisms.

00:07:36.820 --> 00:07:42.420
Suppressive attention attends to the negated phrase and dampens related concept.

00:07:42.980 --> 00:07:47.820
Constructive mechanisms build a representation of the full negated concept.

00:07:47.820 --> 00:07:53.420
Ablating late layer attention shortcuts dramatically improves negation accuracy.

00:07:54.080 --> 00:07:59.540
Poor performance often stems from simple heuristics rather than missing capability.

00:08:00.260 --> 00:08:08.900
A new 3,582 page benchmark evaluates semi-structured information extraction from OCR clinical reports.

00:08:09.460 --> 00:08:12.620
It uses unknown keys and realistic noise.

00:08:13.340 --> 00:08:20.640
Encoder only models surprisingly outperform much larger decoder only models on key condition QA.

00:08:21.360 --> 00:08:27.260
fine-tuned decoder only models win on end-to-end extraction when scale is not controlled.

00:08:30.140 --> 00:08:34.760
This applies a practical baseline for anyone working with medical document pipelines.

00:08:35.620 --> 00:08:39.960
Now let's pop the hood on how language models actually implement negation.

00:08:40.840 --> 00:08:46.280
Everyone talks about the model understands negation as if it were a single learned skill.

00:08:46.920 --> 00:08:53.540
In practice, models implement at least two competing mechanisms that coexist inside the same network.

00:08:54.320 --> 00:08:56.600
The first mechanism is suppressive.

00:08:57.460 --> 00:09:01.560
Specific attention heads learn to attend to the phrase being negated.

00:09:02.000 --> 00:09:04.860
They dampen related concepts downstream.

00:09:05.580 --> 00:09:07.800
The second mechanism is constructive.

00:09:08.320 --> 00:09:12.300
The model builds an explicit representation of the negated state.

00:09:12.920 --> 00:09:18.740
For example, turning not gas into a vector that promotes liquids and solids.

00:09:19.480 --> 00:09:24.120
Ablation studies show the constructive root is more prominent in the models examined.

00:09:24.920 --> 00:09:26.860
Yet both mechanisms are present.

00:09:27.820 --> 00:09:33.660
Late layer attention often overrides the correct internal representation with a simple shortcut.

00:09:34.380 --> 00:09:36.120
That produces the wrong answer.

00:09:36.720 --> 00:09:43.240
Removing those heads can raise negation accuracy dramatically even though the knowledge was already there.

00:09:44.000 --> 00:09:48.200
The effect is strongest in mid layers and fades toward the output.

00:09:49.020 --> 00:09:54.060
This explains why prompting tricks that force step-by-step reasoning sometimes help.

00:09:54.960 --> 00:10:01.160
The practical takeaway is that negation failures are frequently fixable with targeted interventions.

00:10:01.960 --> 00:10:04.960
You do not necessarily need more data or scale.

00:10:05.700 --> 00:10:09.720
Teams hitting negation bugs should first check whether late layer attention

00:10:09.720 --> 00:10:12.680
is the culprit before reaching for fine tuning.

